A buffer returns:
[A00, A01]    // Row 0
[A10, A11]    // Row 1


The controller must eventually produce:
Cycle T0: A00, invalid
Cycle T1: A01, A10
Cycle T2: invalid, A11


The same principle applies to the B columns.

There are two possible implementation approaches:
1. Preload and stage: Read all A rows and B columns into controller-local storage, then start the systolic schedule.
2. Overlapped fetching: Fetch vectors while other vectors are being serialised, using staging registers and independent serializers.

I recommend approach 1 for our first robust implementation. It is easier to verify, makes the timing deterministic, and avoids mixing buffer-fetch control with systolic scheduling.
The trade-off is additional local storage of approximately:
M K + K N

operand elements. We can optimise that later if FPGA resources become a concern.
